Problem:
A coconut seller has to transport coconuts using either rickshaws or ox carts. The rickshaws can take 50 coconuts each and cost $10 per trip. The ox carts can take 30 coconuts each and cost $8 per trip. The seller has at most $200 to spend on transporting the coconuts. Due to pollution, the number of rickshaws must not exceed the number of ox carts. Formulate a LP to maximize the number of coconuts that can be transported.

Model and facts:
{
 "model": {
  "variables": [
   {
    "name": "rickshaw trips",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": true
   },
   {
    "name": "ox cart trips",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": true
   }
  ],
  "objective": {
   "sense": "max",
   "terms": [
    {
     "var": "rickshaw trips",
     "coef": 50.0
    },
    {
     "var": "ox cart trips",
     "coef": 30.0
    }
   ]
  },
  "constraints": [
   {
    "name": "budget",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "rickshaw trips",
      "coef": 10.0
     },
     {
      "var": "ox cart trips",
      "coef": 8.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 200.0
   },
   {
    "name": "pollution",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "rickshaw trips",
      "coef": 1.0
     },
     {
      "var": "ox cart trips",
      "coef": -1.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 0.0
   }
  ]
 },
 "facts": {
  "objective": 880.0,
  "values": {
   "rickshaw trips": 11.0,
   "ox cart trips": 11.0
  },
  "binding": [
   "pollution"
  ],
  "slack": {
   "budget": 2.0,
   "pollution": 0.0
  },
  "shadow_prices": null
 }
}

Problem:
A furniture store only stocks and sells dining tables and chairs. The profit per dining table is $350 and the profit per chair is $75. There is 500 sq ft of space available and a dining table requires 8 sq ft of floor space while a chair requires 2 sq ft. Because chairs sell in larger quantities, at least 70% of all furniture in the store must be chairs. In terms of capital, a dining table ties up $1000 in capital and a chair ties up $150 in capital. The company wants a maximum of $20000 worth of capital tied up at any time. Formulate an LP to maximize profit.

Model and facts:
{
 "model": {
  "variables": [
   {
    "name": "dining tables",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": true
   },
   {
    "name": "chairs",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": true
   }
  ],
  "objective": {
   "sense": "max",
   "terms": [
    {
     "var": "dining tables",
     "coef": 350.0
    },
    {
     "var": "chairs",
     "coef": 75.0
    }
   ]
  },
  "constraints": [
   {
    "name": "floor space",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "dining tables",
      "coef": 8.0
     },
     {
      "var": "chairs",
      "coef": 2.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 500.0
   },
   {
    "name": "chair share of furniture",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "dining tables",
      "coef": -0.7
     },
     {
      "var": "chairs",
      "coef": 0.3
     }
    ],
    "sense": ">=",
    "rhs": 0.0
   },
   {
    "name": "capital",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "dining tables",
      "coef": 1000.0
     },
     {
      "var": "chairs",
      "coef": 150.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 20000.0
   }
  ]
 },
 "facts": {
  "objective": 9975.0,
  "values": {
   "dining tables": 0.0,
   "chairs": 133.0
  },
  "binding": [],
  "slack": {
   "floor space": 234.0,
   "chair share of furniture": 39.9,
   "capital": 50.0
  },
  "shadow_prices": null
 }
}

Problem:
A bakery sells two types of cakes. They sell a chocolate cake and a vanilla cake. Let's say they make x1 chocolate cakes, at a profit of $10 each, and x2 vanilla cakes, at a profit of $11 each (x1 and x2 are unknowns both greater than or equal to 0). The daily demand for these cakes is at most 50 chocolate cakes and at most 30 vanilla cakes. The bakery is short staffed and can make a maximum of 50 cakes of either type per day. How much of each cake should the bakery make in order to maximize profit?

Model and facts:
{
 "model": {
  "variables": [
   {
    "name": "chocolate cakes",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": true
   },
   {
    "name": "vanilla cakes",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": true
   }
  ],
  "objective": {
   "sense": "max",
   "terms": [
    {
     "var": "chocolate cakes",
     "coef": 10.0
    },
    {
     "var": "vanilla cakes",
     "coef": 11.0
    }
   ]
  },
  "constraints": [
   {
    "name": "chocolate demand",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "chocolate cakes",
      "coef": 1.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 50.0
   },
   {
    "name": "vanilla demand",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "vanilla cakes",
      "coef": 1.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 30.0
   },
   {
    "name": "daily production capacity",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "chocolate cakes",
      "coef": 1.0
     },
     {
      "var": "vanilla cakes",
      "coef": 1.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 50.0
   }
  ]
 },
 "facts": {
  "objective": 530.0,
  "values": {
   "chocolate cakes": 20.0,
   "vanilla cakes": 30.0
  },
  "binding": [
   "vanilla demand",
   "daily production capacity"
  ],
  "slack": {
   "chocolate demand": 30.0,
   "vanilla demand": 0.0,
   "daily production capacity": 0.0
  },
  "shadow_prices": null
 }
}

Problem:
A company wants to advertise its product. They want to use three types of ads: magazine ads, flyers, and billboard ads. The cost for each option along with the expected viewership is given. A magazine ad costs $1000 and attracts 10000 viewers. A flyer costs $200 and attracts 5000 viewers. A billboard ad costs $7000 and attracts 25000 viewers. To avoid annoying customers, the city has limited the number of flyers from a single company to 8. In addition, at most a third of the total number of ads should be billboard ads and at least 15% of the ads should be magazine ads. If the weekly budget is $50000, how many ads of each type should be run in order to maximize the number of viewers?

Model and facts:
{
 "model": {
  "variables": [
   {
    "name": "magazine ads",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": true
   },
   {
    "name": "flyers",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": true
   },
   {
    "name": "billboard ads",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": true
   }
  ],
  "objective": {
   "sense": "max",
   "terms": [
    {
     "var": "magazine ads",
     "coef": 10000.0
    },
    {
     "var": "flyers",
     "coef": 5000.0
    },
    {
     "var": "billboard ads",
     "coef": 25000.0
    }
   ]
  },
  "constraints": [
   {
    "name": "budget",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "magazine ads",
      "coef": 1000.0
     },
     {
      "var": "flyers",
      "coef": 200.0
     },
     {
      "var": "billboard ads",
      "coef": 7000.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 50000.0
   },
   {
    "name": "flyer_limit",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "flyers",
      "coef": 1.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 8.0
   },
   {
    "name": "billboard_share",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "billboard ads",
      "coef": 2.0
     },
     {
      "var": "magazine ads",
      "coef": -1.0
     },
     {
      "var": "flyers",
      "coef": -1.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 0.0
   },
   {
    "name": "magazine_share",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "magazine ads",
      "coef": 0.85
     },
     {
      "var": "flyers",
      "coef": -0.15
     },
     {
      "var": "billboard ads",
      "coef": -0.15
     }
    ],
    "sense": ">=",
    "rhs": 0.0
   }
  ]
 },
 "facts": {
  "objective": 520000.0,
  "values": {
   "magazine ads": 48.0,
   "flyers": 8.0,
   "billboard ads": 0.0
  },
  "binding": [
   "flyer_limit"
  ],
  "slack": {
   "budget": 400.0,
   "flyer_limit": 0.0,
   "billboard_share": 56.0,
   "magazine_share": 39.6
  },
  "shadow_prices": null
 }
}

Problem:
A spicy paste is to be made from units of peppers and tomatoes and is to contain a minimum of 130 units of spice and 90 units of flavor. Peppers cost $8 per unit and tomatoes cost $5 per unit. One unit of peppers contains 12 units of spice and 2 units of flavor. One unit of tomatoes contains 1 unit of spice and 8 units of flavor. Formulate this as an LP and find the minimum cost spicy paste that can be made.

Model and facts:
{
 "model": {
  "variables": [
   {
    "name": "peppers units",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": false
   },
   {
    "name": "tomato units",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": false
   }
  ],
  "objective": {
   "sense": "min",
   "terms": [
    {
     "var": "peppers units",
     "coef": 8.0
    },
    {
     "var": "tomato units",
     "coef": 5.0
    }
   ]
  },
  "constraints": [
   {
    "name": "spice minimum",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "peppers units",
      "coef": 12.0
     },
     {
      "var": "tomato units",
      "coef": 1.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": ">=",
    "rhs": 130.0
   },
   {
    "name": "flavor minimum",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "peppers units",
      "coef": 2.0
     },
     {
      "var": "tomato units",
      "coef": 8.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": ">=",
    "rhs": 90.0
   }
  ]
 },
 "facts": {
  "objective": 124.468086,
  "values": {
   "peppers units": 10.106383,
   "tomato units": 8.723404
  },
  "binding": [
   "spice minimum",
   "flavor minimum"
  ],
  "slack": {
   "spice minimum": 0.0,
   "flavor minimum": 0.0
  },
  "shadow_prices": {
   "spice minimum": 0.574468,
   "flavor minimum": 0.553191
  }
 }
}

Problem:
A suit store makes 2-piece and 3-piece suits. To make a 2-piece suit requires 1 hour of measuring and 4 hours of stitching. To make a 3-piece suit requires 2 hours of measuring and 5 hours of stitching. The store has 150 hours of measuring time and 300 hours of stitching time available. In order to stay in business, at least 40 2-piece suites should be made. The profit per 2-piece suit is $600 and the profit per 3-piece suit is $800. Formulate a LP to maximize profit?

Model and facts:
{
 "model": {
  "variables": [
   {
    "name": "2-piece suits",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": true
   },
   {
    "name": "3-piece suits",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": true
   }
  ],
  "objective": {
   "sense": "max",
   "terms": [
    {
     "var": "2-piece suits",
     "coef": 600.0
    },
    {
     "var": "3-piece suits",
     "coef": 800.0
    }
   ]
  },
  "constraints": [
   {
    "name": "measuring_hours",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "2-piece suits",
      "coef": 1.0
     },
     {
      "var": "3-piece suits",
      "coef": 2.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 150.0
   },
   {
    "name": "stitching_hours",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "2-piece suits",
      "coef": 4.0
     },
     {
      "var": "3-piece suits",
      "coef": 5.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 300.0
   },
   {
    "name": "min_two_piece_suits",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "2-piece suits",
      "coef": 1.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": ">=",
    "rhs": 40.0
   }
  ]
 },
 "facts": {
  "objective": 46400.0,
  "values": {
   "2-piece suits": 40.0,
   "3-piece suits": 28.0
  },
  "binding": [
   "stitching_hours",
   "min_two_piece_suits"
  ],
  "slack": {
   "measuring_hours": 54.0,
   "stitching_hours": 0.0,
   "min_two_piece_suits": 0.0
  },
  "shadow_prices": null
 }
}

Problem:
The Chips Alloy company produces 150 tons of iron ore and 35 tons of zinc ore each month. These can be treated in different ways to produce three different alloy grades: Standard, Plus or Premium. To produce 1 ton of the Standard grade alloy requires 3 tons of iron ore and 1 ton of zinc ore. To produce 1 ton of the Plus grade alloy requires 5 tons of iron ore and 2 tons of zinc ore. Finally, to produce 1 ton of the Premium grade alloy requires 6 tons of iron ore and 3 tons of zinc ore. The profit per ton from selling the alloys are $1500, $2500 and $5500 for the Standard, Plus, and Premium grades respectively. How much of each alloy grade should be produced to maximize profit?

Model and facts:
{
 "model": {
  "variables": [
   {
    "name": "standard_alloy_tons",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": false
   },
   {
    "name": "plus_alloy_tons",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": false
   },
   {
    "name": "premium_alloy_tons",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": false
   }
  ],
  "objective": {
   "sense": "max",
   "terms": [
    {
     "var": "standard_alloy_tons",
     "coef": 1500.0
    },
    {
     "var": "plus_alloy_tons",
     "coef": 2500.0
    },
    {
     "var": "premium_alloy_tons",
     "coef": 5500.0
    }
   ]
  },
  "constraints": [
   {
    "name": "iron_ore_available",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "standard_alloy_tons",
      "coef": 3.0
     },
     {
      "var": "plus_alloy_tons",
      "coef": 5.0
     },
     {
      "var": "premium_alloy_tons",
      "coef": 6.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 150.0
   },
   {
    "name": "zinc_ore_available",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "standard_alloy_tons",
      "coef": 1.0
     },
     {
      "var": "plus_alloy_tons",
      "coef": 2.0
     },
     {
      "var": "premium_alloy_tons",
      "coef": 3.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 35.0
   }
  ]
 },
 "facts": {
  "objective": 64166.6685,
  "values": {
   "standard_alloy_tons": 0.0,
   "plus_alloy_tons": 0.0,
   "premium_alloy_tons": 11.666667
  },
  "binding": [
   "zinc_ore_available"
  ],
  "slack": {
   "iron_ore_available": 79.999998,
   "zinc_ore_available": -1e-06
  },
  "shadow_prices": {
   "iron_ore_available": 0.0,
   "zinc_ore_available": 1833.3333
  }
 }
}

Problem:
A kitchen prepares diced onions and carrots each involving both peeling and cutting. To prepare a container of diced onions takes 10 minutes of peeling and 20 minutes of cutting. To prepare a container of diced carrots takes 20 minutes of peeling and 15 minutes of cutting. There are 2000 minutes of peeling time and 2500 minutes of cutting time available. If the profit per container of diced onions is $8 and the profit per container of diced carrots is $6, how many containers of each should be prepared to maximize profit?

Model and facts:
{
 "model": {
  "variables": [
   {
    "name": "containers of diced onions",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": true
   },
   {
    "name": "containers of diced carrots",
    "lower": 0.0,
    "upper": null,
    "integer": true
   }
  ],
  "objective": {
   "sense": "max",
   "terms": [
    {
     "var": "containers of diced onions",
     "coef": 8.0
    },
    {
     "var": "containers of diced carrots",
     "coef": 6.0
    }
   ]
  },
  "constraints": [
   {
    "name": "peeling time",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "containers of diced onions",
      "coef": 10.0
     },
     {
      "var": "containers of diced carrots",
      "coef": 20.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 2000.0
   },
   {
    "name": "cutting time",
    "terms": [
     {
      "var": "containers of diced onions",
      "coef": 20.0
     },
     {
      "var": "containers of diced carrots",
      "coef": 15.0
     }
    ],
    "sense": "<=",
    "rhs": 2500.0
   }
  ]
 },
 "facts": {
  "objective": 1000.0,
  "values": {
   "containers of diced onions": 80.0,
   "containers of diced carrots": 60.0
  },
  "binding": [
   "peeling time",
   "cutting time"
  ],
  "slack": {
   "peeling time": 0.0,
   "cutting time": 0.0
  },
  "shadow_prices": null
 }
}

Problem:
A cell-phone company makes foldable and regular cell-phones in a factory that is divided into a hardware and software section. The hardware section must work 10 hours on each foldable cell-phone but only 3 hours on each regular one. The software section must work 3 hours for each cell-phone. The profit from the foldable cell-phone is $500 and the profit from the regular cell-phone is $300. Due to a recent strike, the hardware section only has 200 hours available per week and the software section only has 150 hours available per week. How many of each phone should the company produce to maximize profit?

